WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.802
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy and Tony

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.370
Award-winning actor
you know from movies like

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.639
"Moulin Rouge!",
"Ice Age," and "Encanto."

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.975
He stars in "The Menu,"
which is in theaters

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:10.577
November 18th.
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:13.012
-Do you need assistance
finding your seat?

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:16.048
-There's a finger right there.
-The man is screaming in pain.

00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:17.416
-Please. Sit.

00:00:17.416 --> 00:00:18.718
[ Man sobbing ]

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.754
-Not funny, lady.
Jesus Christ.

00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:23.890
-It's all part of the menu.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.959
-Oh, my God.
-This is real, isn't it?

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:28.461
Can't do it. I can't --
I can't do a hostage --

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:30.429
-Then why don't you
go talk to him?

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:32.431
Go talk to him.
Because you know him, right?

00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:35.535
-I made that up.
-Why?

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:38.705
-Because I'm a name-dropping
whore. That's why, okay?

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:40.606
-Please welcome back
to the show our friend

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:42.542
John Leguizamo, everybody.

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:44.210
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:50.016 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:55.821 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:55.821 --> 00:00:58.624
Welcome back.
-Thank you for having me back.

00:00:58.624 --> 00:01:00.126
-I was lucky enough
to see you on Monday

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:02.061
at the premiere of this movie,
which I loved a great deal.

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:03.730
One of our writers here
at "Late Night"

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.832
was a co-writer on this film.
Seth Reiss.

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:07.133
-Seth Reiss.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.768
-You got to meet Seth
and work with Seth.

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.136
How was your time with him?
-A lot of time with him.

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:11.738
Well, he wrote
this character for me --

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:14.440
this egotistical actor.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.409
And he knows about egotistical,
you know,

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.410
because you know Seth himself.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:19.845
He's kind of egotistical.

00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:21.880
-Oh, I thought you were saying
he knows egotistical

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:23.549
because he writes for me.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:25.652
-[ Laughs ]
-No.

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:27.052
-Very self-revealing of you.

00:01:27.052 --> 00:01:29.088
-You get to be -- You do play
an egotistical actor.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:30.856
Did you base it on anything,

00:01:30.856 --> 00:01:32.825
actors that you've worked with
over the years?

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:34.593
-You know, it's funny
that you should ask that

00:01:34.593 --> 00:01:37.396
because I play
a washed-up action star.

00:01:37.396 --> 00:01:39.165
And I've worked up
with a lot of action stars

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:41.134
that are washed-up now.

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:43.603
And there was one
in particular that I --

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:47.473
that I like to use,
and that's Steven Seagal.

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:49.075
-Oh, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:51.377
So that's who I based it on.
-That's great.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.446
I kind of want to go watch it
a second time now.

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:55.915
-You see that, yeah. The hair.
I did the hair kind of like him.

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:57.383
Painted scalp
and stuff like that.

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:59.318
-Did you actually -- Did you
work with him on a movie?

00:01:59.318 --> 00:02:01.321
-Yes, I did.
"Executive Decision."

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:02.421
-Okay.

00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:03.556
-And I was playing
his right-hand man,

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:05.124
and he knocked me out
in rehearsals.

00:02:05.124 --> 00:02:07.026
-Oh, wow. By accident?

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:09.762
-Oh no, no. On purpose.
-Wow!

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:11.930
-Because I was laughing at him,
'cause -- As I tend to do.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.400
I do laugh at people.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.335
I don't take anybody seriously,
not myself.

00:02:16.335 --> 00:02:17.737
But I was laughing at him,

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:21.007
and he elbowed me so hard
in the solar plexus.

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:22.508
Knocked me against the wall.

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:25.377
I hit the wall,
and I slid down and --

00:02:25.377 --> 00:02:26.679
-Wow!
-Yeah.

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:29.515
But he dies in the movie.
So I was there the first --

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:30.983
I was the first one there
to see him die.

00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:33.519
Even though it was fake,
I still --

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:35.488
-It's very nice for you
to come and watch

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:37.957
while other actors work, yeah.

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:42.094
This is a great film.
You obviously are working --

00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:44.163
I think the first time
you were on the show,

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:46.699
I talked about, you know,
I know you from one-man shows.

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:48.801
That was the first time
I saw you.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:51.938
And now you're doing a new one
that is actually going to be

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:54.073
a musical one-man show,
which I'm --

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:56.308
-It's not a one-man show.
I'm sorry.

00:02:56.308 --> 00:02:58.444
"Kiss my Aztec"
is my new musical.

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.513
-Okay, gotcha. "Kiss my Aztec."
All right.

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:01.814
-But I can't sing.

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:04.016
-Oh, so that's why you need
more man in the show?

00:03:04.016 --> 00:03:05.485
-Yeah, exactly.

00:03:05.485 --> 00:03:07.920
I need a lot more people in
the show because I can't sing.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.088
I mean, I've got a great voice.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:10.523
I just can't hit
a melody or a pitch.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.491
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-It's a beautiful voice.

00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:15.394
And, yeah, it's coming
to Broadway next year, yeah.

00:03:15.394 --> 00:03:18.764
It's a real funny take
on conquest and genocide.

00:03:18.764 --> 00:03:20.500
-Oh, that's good. Well --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.134
-Fodder. Fodder for humor.
Yeah.

00:03:22.134 --> 00:03:23.636
-If there's any pair of hands

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.471
I feel like that might be
safe in, it's yours.

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:26.939
-Yeah, yeah, no, I handle it.
I handle it.

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:29.508
-You were Bruno in "Encanto."
-Yes, yes. Thank you.

00:03:29.508 --> 00:03:31.244
[ Cheers and applause ]
Oh, thank you.

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:33.112
Thank you.
-Now, you know,

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:35.781
obviously you've done
animated films before.

00:03:35.781 --> 00:03:38.784
But to be the sort of --
the title character in the song

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:41.387
that was what everybody was
singing for the course of --

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:42.955
-It was a crazy hit.
-What was it like?

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:44.624
-Oh, my God. It was incredible.

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:46.058
First, Lin-Manuel
calls me up and goes,

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:48.027
"John, I wrote a rap for you."

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:49.128
And I go,
"Oh, my God, he wrote a rap!

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:50.563
I'm going to be in a movie!"

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.932
Then the poor guy suffered
because he had to spend hours

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:55.034
and hours trying
to get me into pocket.

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:58.204
Because I cannot sing.
So he suffered a lot.

00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:00.973
But you know,
parents recognize me,

00:04:00.973 --> 00:04:03.575
and then they, like, want me
to meet their kids and go,

00:04:03.575 --> 00:04:05.545
"This is Bruno,"
and then it never goes well.

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:07.146
-The kids --
-They don't get it.

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:09.014
-They don't get it.
They don't like it.

00:04:09.014 --> 00:04:10.349
-They don't like it.

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:13.552
They see this mug and they go,
"Oh, no, not so cool.

00:04:13.552 --> 00:04:17.289
He's not really Bruno."
-Yeah. It is really funny.

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:19.325
They're like,
"You know the clean lines,

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:21.861
the clean animation lines
of your beloved character?

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:23.595
He's actually a real grown man."

00:04:23.595 --> 00:04:27.566
-Yeah. With whiskers.
Funny-looking man.

00:04:27.566 --> 00:04:30.302
-You have this movie
I've been seeing trailers for

00:04:30.302 --> 00:04:32.104
with the great David Harbour
called --

00:04:32.104 --> 00:04:33.872 line:0%
-Oh, my God. He's so good.
-..."Violent Night."

00:04:33.872 --> 00:04:35.941 line:0%
This is coming out. This seems
like the kind of Christmas

00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:37.309 line:0%
film that
we haven't seen before.

00:04:37.309 --> 00:04:38.744 line:0%
-Oh, hell yeah.

00:04:38.744 --> 00:04:42.281 line:0%
This is an anti-Christmas movie
for Christmas.

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:45.550
Like, people who hate Christmas
movies, this is your movie.

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:47.420
Because he's a badass.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.623
He drinks. He curses.
He punches people out.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:53.025
He pisses from his reindeer --
flying reindeer.

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:54.026
-Gotcha.

00:04:54.026 --> 00:04:55.528
The Santa
we've been waiting for.

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:56.963
-Yes.
-The Santa For this era.

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:59.165
And I'm going to go on a limb
and say David --

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:01.834
a better action movie costar
than Steven Seagal?

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:05.304
-Oh, he never hurt me.
We had a big fight sequence.

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:07.373
50-punch fight sequence,
and he never hurt me.

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:09.074
I love that man.
-That's a gentleman.

00:05:09.074 --> 00:05:10.276
-Yes, sir.

00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:12.345
-You know, in "The Menu,"
you play an actor

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.547
who's about to embark on
doing a reality show,

00:05:14.547 --> 00:05:17.616
which you're actually doing
in real life.

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:19.085
John Leguizamo.
What's the show?

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:20.620
It's going to be on MSNBC,
right?

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.488
-It was a little weird
because -- you know,

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:24.156
It's a washed-up movie star

00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:26.725
who's using the Food Network
to revitalize his career.

00:05:26.725 --> 00:05:30.496
Then -- "Wait a minute. I'm
working on a Food Network show."

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:31.764
Is my career washed-up?

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:33.632
I mean, I was never
an action star,

00:05:33.632 --> 00:05:35.734
so I can't be
a washed-up action star.

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:39.004
Anyway, yes. Anyway, but this
is beautiful, for MSNBC.

00:05:39.004 --> 00:05:40.205
It's about Latin culture.

00:05:40.205 --> 00:05:42.207
And I go across
all of America --

00:05:42.207 --> 00:05:44.543
a docuseries,
exploring Latin culture.

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:48.347
Chefs, singers, artists,
activists, politicians.

00:05:48.347 --> 00:05:50.449
-Have you shot it yet?
-Yes, we shot six episodes.

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:51.851
-Now, I have to ask you this.

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:54.353
Because I did exactly
one episode of one travel show.

00:05:54.353 --> 00:05:56.555
-And what happened?
-It was the most exhausting --

00:05:56.555 --> 00:05:58.357
I think they look so fun.
-Oh, my God.

00:05:58.357 --> 00:06:00.326
-It's so tiring, right?
-Oh, you said it.

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:02.027
My God.
It's the most exhausting --

00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:03.796
-You're so full all the time.

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:06.432
They make you eat so much.
-And you got to keep eating

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:08.334
because sometimes
you make mistakes,

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.369
so now I gotta eat
a whole new what?

00:06:10.369 --> 00:06:12.004
That didn't come out right?

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:14.773
I got to eat that whole
lechon again? Oh, my God.

00:06:14.773 --> 00:06:16.041
And you got to enjoy it.

00:06:16.041 --> 00:06:17.376
-All things considered,
that's why --

00:06:17.376 --> 00:06:18.710
I'm surprised
you've already done it,

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:20.479
because I assumed you'd be
a full 100 pounds heavier.

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:22.681
-Oh, I was 100 pounds heavier.
-You just worked it off?

00:06:22.681 --> 00:06:24.350
-Yeah, yeah, I've been fasting

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:27.386
since they shot it,
been fasting for six months.

00:06:27.386 --> 00:06:28.620
-We talked about, you know,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.223
obviously both a film actor
and a stage actor.

00:06:30.223 --> 00:06:32.825
In your early days, you loved
the idea of going to shows.

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:35.928
Didn't quite have the money
to do it. How do --

00:06:35.928 --> 00:06:38.264
Explain how you would find
your way into Broadway theaters.

00:06:38.264 --> 00:06:39.999
-Yeah, well, you know,
I was broke, I was ghetto.

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:42.167
I was -- you know,
but I loved theater.

00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:45.104
But it was unaffordable
for a poor guy like myself.

00:06:45.104 --> 00:06:47.406
So I would do this thing
called "second acting."

00:06:47.406 --> 00:06:48.840
Have you ever done that?
-No.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:51.076
-I guess -- Well, now people
will know how to do it.

00:06:51.076 --> 00:06:54.246
Back then, there was none
of that digital-ticket stuff,

00:06:54.246 --> 00:06:55.614
and people smoked a lot,

00:06:55.614 --> 00:06:57.683
so I would wait
for the second act

00:06:57.683 --> 00:06:59.719
when all the smokers
would come out,

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:02.687
and I would dress up
with my best polyester suit,

00:07:02.687 --> 00:07:04.523
and I'd bring cigarettes.

00:07:04.523 --> 00:07:07.426
I was too young to smoke,
but I would smoke anyway.

00:07:07.426 --> 00:07:09.695
And I'd grab a playbill
that was on the ground.

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:11.497
And you run in.
-Ah!

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:13.065
-With all the smokers,
and you run in.

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:15.401
And I look down,
and then you go to the bathroom,

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:17.036
and you wait for everybody
to sit down.

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:19.805
And then you run back out
and you sit in the empty seats.

00:07:19.805 --> 00:07:22.307
-That's amazing.
-And I saw "Elephant Man."

00:07:22.307 --> 00:07:25.644
I saw "Waiting for Godot."
I never saw the first act.

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:27.346
Which is probably better
for "Waiting for Godot."

00:07:27.346 --> 00:07:29.348
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
I mean, ultimately --

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:31.984
It's still just
a lot of waiting.

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:34.353
-Always. Yeah.
Existential waiting.

00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:36.222
-During the second act
of "Waiting for Godot,"

00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:37.923
did you turn
to other people and go, like,

00:07:37.923 --> 00:07:39.525
"Did anything happen
in the first act?"

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:41.160
-I was always afraid
of talking to people

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.761
because they'd turn me in
or something.

00:07:42.761 --> 00:07:45.264
-Yeah. Well, a very novel idea.

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:46.699
Obviously, it paid off
down the line.

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:48.801
And thank you for being here.
It's always such a pleasure.

00:07:48.801 --> 00:07:50.302
-Thank you for having me,
brother.

00:07:50.302 --> 00:07:52.104
-"The Menu"
is in theaters this Friday.

00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:54.240
And "Violent Night"
opens December 2nd.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.409
We'll be right back
with Zoe Kazan!

